{{Politics of Balisca}}

'''Shadow authoritarianism''' ([[Baliscano]]: ''Autoritarismo sombra''), also called '''invisible authoritarianism''' (''autoritarismo invisible''), or simply the '''shadow state''' (''estau sombra''), is a right-wing {{W|conspiracy theory}} which [[W:Hypothesis|hypothesizes]] that the [[United Left]], in a "{{W|power behind the throne}}" fashion, is actually an [[W:Authoritarianism|authoritarian]] {{W|shadow government (conspiracy)|shadow government}} with conspiring to eventually established an {{W|One-party system|one-party}} authoritarian regime in [[Balisca]].

The common theme in conspiracy theories about shadow authoritarianism is that a [[W:shadow government (conspiracy)|secretive]] power {{W|elite}} with a {{W|Internationalism|internationalist}} agenda is conspiring to eventually rule the country through an {{W|authoritarian}} one-party government—dedicated to establishing "[[W:Communist society|full communism]]" with {{W|Techno-utopianism|the help of technology}}—and an all-encompassing {{W|propaganda}} whose ideology hails the implementation of [[accelerationism]] as the culmination of Balisca's post-revolutionary progress. Many influential [[Baliscan Revolution|post-Revolution]] historical and contemporary figures have therefore been alleged to be part of a cabal that operates through many {{W|front organizations}}, like the [[Baliscan Selective Service|Selective Service]] and the [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation councils]] to orchestrate significant political and financial events, ranging from causing systemic crises to pushing through controversial policies, at local, confederal and international levels, as steps in an ongoing plot to circumvent [[Basic Laws of Balisca|constitutional safeguards]] and achieve political domination. It is also alleged by the theories' supporters that the government heavily utilizes {{W|egalitarianism|egalitarian}} and {{W|left-wing populism|left-wing populist}} rhetoric to shape public opinion in its favor ({{W|virtue signalling}}) by portraying itself as {{W|political correctness|politically correct}}, {{W|inclusive}}, {{W|cosmopolitan}} and morally superior through benevolent action in the face of {{W|culture wars}} and international increases in popularity of {{W|right-wing populism}}. 

It is also stated that the confederal administration and it's security arms ([[Intelligence Service (Balisca)|SI]], [[Social Protection Service|SPS]]) attempt to preempt {{W|dissent|opposition}} and control {{W|dissident|critic}}s through {{W|covert}} means, using secret methods of [[W:Abusive power and control|abusive control]] and {{W|psychological manipulation}} to discourage "anti-cosmopolitan" activities. People are allegedly targeted on a preventative basis, for politically incorrect activities they may have gone on to perform, and not on the basis of crimes they had actually committed. 

First emerging as part of the [[First Red Scare]] and spread by [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|Baliscan exiles]], the shadow authoritarianism theory was limited to militant anti-leftist groups outside of the [[Baliscan archipelago]], the underground Baliscan far-right (like [[Triple A (Balisca)|Triple A]]), and anti-government youth countercultures. Skeptics have observed that [[W:right-wing populism|right-wing populist]] conspiracy theories about a shadow authoritarian state had seeped into popular [[W:Western media|Western media]] during and after the [[W:Cold War|Cold War]], correlating to a period where people were gaining access to information with little fact-checking or sources, leaving them susceptible to {{W|misinformation}}. Think tanks, confederal officials, and political scientists have all voiced concerns that the potential {{W|mass hysteria}} over these conspiracy theories could eventually have negative effects on Baliscan social life, ranging from the possibility of [[W:lone wolf (terrorism)|lone-wolf terrorism]] to the rise to power of authoritarian {{W|demagogue}}s and {{w|civil unrest}} and {{W|political violence}}.

This conspiracy theory has been roundly condemned by countless historians, fact-checkers, and political scientists across the political spectrum. Additionally, it has been disparaged across Baliscan social media, but has rapidly gained traction internationally, especially in the {{W|Western world}} since the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}, the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]] and the success of the [[2022 Baliscan constitutional referendum]].

